# Every text file is stored in the repository with LF endings, whatever the
# editor that saved it did locally.
#
# This is not housekeeping. Two concrete things go wrong without it:
#
#   1. A Windows editor that rewrites a file with CRLF makes git report every
#      single line as changed. `.github/workflows/predictions.yml` and
#      `.gitignore` were sitting in exactly that state when this file was added —
#      195 lines "modified" with byte-identical content, staged for the next
#      commit. Nothing had been committed that way yet, and this is what keeps it
#      that way: the archive's whole claim rests on its diffs being readable, and
#      a real one-line change is invisible inside a whole-file rewrite.
#
#   2. The workflow's `run:` blocks are bash executed on Ubuntu. A CRLF line
#      ending inside a shell script leaves a stray carriage return on the end of
#      every command, which produces errors like `$'\r': command not found` —
#      genuinely broken, not cosmetic, and mystifying to read.
#
# If a file ever does need its endings preserved, give it its own line here
# rather than removing this one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Nothing in Prediq is binary today — team crests are fetched from ESPN at run
# time rather than committed. These are here so that if an image or font is ever
# added, git does not try to normalise line endings inside it and corrupt it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
